0	section	Sergeant Hudson
1	line	Player	What do I need to be doing?
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	You don't need to be doing anything. We have everything in hand here.
1	line	Player	You know what I mean...
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	If you wish to investigate the crime scene, I won't stop you, but I'm not going to waste time telling you how to do it. I'm busy enough as it is.
1	action	end
0	section	Corporal Breiman
1	line	Corporal Breiman	How goes it? You're the one doing some of your own investigating, right?
1	line	Player	That's right.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Pleased to meet you. I'm Corporal Michael Breiman. I work with Corporal Campter and Sergeant Hudson.
1	line	Player	And you guys at the ones who investigate things like this?
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Like murder? Yup, that's our domain. Very cheery.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	We work for the Shayzien Constabulary. It's part of the Shayzien Military. We investigate crimes all across the kingdom.
1	line	Player	I see. What are your thoughts so far?
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Well, it's still a bit early to suggest too much. That said, this one doesn't look good.
1	line	Player	Do any murders look good?
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Well... No, but this one could be particularly nasty. You see, there are generally three kinds of murder.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	First, you've got your petty crime that gets out of hand. Thievery and the like. From time to time, a thief ends up biting off more than they can chew, and the victim fights back.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Both parties panic in that situation, and it sometimes ends badly.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Then you've got your domestic violence. An argument breaks out between the victim and someone close to them and it spirals out of control. It's by far the most common type of murder we see.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Now, the thing about those first two is that the killer never sets out to kill someone. One thing leads to another and then there's a body on the floor. That's what's so dangerous about the third kind.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	You see, with the third kind of murder, the killer wants the victim dead. They plan for it, sometimes for months. They set out with the intention of ending someone's life.
1	line	Player	And you think that's what we're dealing with here?
1	line	Corporal Breiman	It definitely looks that way. Our victim here was a bit of a public figure before he retired. I even remember hearing stories about him when I was a kid.
1	line	Corporal Breiman	That, combined with the lovely decoration on the wall, and the way the victim's been dismembered... Well, it doesn't paint a good picture...
1	line	Corporal Breiman	Anyway, I'll let you get on with your investigation. If you need anything, just let me or my partner know. She should be outside somewhere.
1	line	Player	Will do. Thanks.
1	action	end
0	section	Corporal Campter
1	line	Player	Hello there.
1	line	Corporal Campter	Hello, citizen. I hear you're going to be doing some investigating with us.
1	line	Player	That's right. Not that your boss seemed very happy about it.
1	line	Corporal Campter	Ah, don't worry about him. He can be a bit short with people sometimes. It's how he is when he's in his thinking mode.
1	line	Player	Thinking mode?
1	line	Corporal Campter	That's right. He's the brains of the operation you see. I'm Corporal Sarah Campter. It's my job, along with my partner, to gather up any evidence and interview the witnesses.
1	line	Corporal Campter	Once we've done that, we present it to the Sergeant and he pieces it all together. He's caught more killers than you could count!
1	line	Player	I see. So, any tips for my investigation?
1	line	Corporal Campter	Well, when it comes to murder, there are a few things we generally look at. First, there's the body itself. Determining the cause of death is a good start for any investigation.
1	line	Corporal Campter	After that, there's searching the crime scene, or any other areas of interest. We look for anything the killer might have left behind, but also possible hints at a motive that could be hidden amongst the victim's possessions.
1	line	Corporal Campter	Finally, the witnesses. We'll interview anyone who we think might have seen something, or who might have had contact with the victim before their death. Neighbours... family... Those kinds of people,
1	line	Corporal Campter	They can often fill in critical gaps in the evidence, but be careful. Even when not guilty, people have a habit of telling lies.
1	line	Player	I see. Thanks for the help.
1	line	Corporal Campter	Don't mention it. If you need anything else, my partner should be over in the house. He might have some useful information for you.
1	action	end
