0	section	Inspecting the blood in the lair
1	line	Familiar Voice	Get up.
1	line	Betty	I said get up.
1	line	Player	Betty...? What is this...?
1	line	Betty	You should know what this is... You investigated it. All those clues... and you still couldn't work it out.
1	line	Player	It was... It was you!
1	line	Betty	Of course it was me. Who else could it have been?
1	line	Player	But... your own father... Why?
1	line	Betty	Because he was a monster! For my entire childhood we lived in fear of him, until the day finally came when my mother decided enough was enough...
1	line	Betty	But he couldn't allow that! He couldn't let her be free. That's why he poisoned her! Her and my sister!
1	line	Betty	Then, with a little help from his Keystone Lodge friends, he had it all covered up!
1	line	Betty	But now, after all these years, it's finally over! They're finally dead! All that remains now, is you.
1	line	Player	Me? Why? I tried to help you!
1	line	Betty	But you never once looked deeper! Don't you see? You're just as big a part of the problem as they were!
1	line	Betty	People like them... the only reason they get away with the things they do is because people like you stand by and let them!
1	line	Betty	You never really cared! You people never do! This was all just a bit of fun to you. But now the fun is over...
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Indeed it is.
1	line	Betty	What...? How?!
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	By following the clues, Betty. Isn't that what you wanted?
1	line	Betty	But you never even cared!
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Oh I very much did. That's why one of the first things I did was look into the death of your mother and sister. The investigation into their deaths was quite thorough...
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	The conclusion? An illness. One so tragic, the surviving daughter never fully recovered. She spent years in care, but eventually her father decided to bring her home.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	He was advised against it. She was unpredictable. Sometimes even violent. But he insisted.
1	line	Betty	You're lying!
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	The people you killed were innocent, Betty. No one is to blame for what happened to your mother and sister. You've just never been able to accept that.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Now, I think it's time to bring this madness to an end. You're coming with us, Betty.
1	line	Betty	So you can execute me in front of a cheering crowd? Or lock me in a cell to live the rest of my life with no sunlight and a diet of mouldy bread? No. This ends here!
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	*sigh*
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Come on, adventurer, let's get you out of here. Corporals... get started on cleaning up here.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Well... This one is going to have some paperwork to deal with...
1	line	Player	When did you realise?
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Hmm?
1	line	Player	That it was Betty.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	She was a prime suspect from the start. Such is always the case when dealing with a murder. In the vast majority of cases, the killer and the victim know each other.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	As for when I knew for sure, I had a strong hunch, but it was just that until you led us to her.
1	line	Player	It would have been nice of you to tell me...
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Yes, it would have been, but through your own choices, you made yourself a suspect as well. It's nothing personal, but that is the way of things I'm afraid.
1	line	Player	I suppose...
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	If it makes you feel better, your work did end up being useful. You found a couple of things we missed, and you did a good job piecing everything together.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	I can't offer you much as thanks, but I do have an amulet here that might be useful to you.
1	line	Sergeant Hudson	Now, I'd best get to work. We have a lot of tidying up to do. All the best, adventurer.
1	box	Sergeant Hudson departs.
